Neutral primordial gas at n_H = 1 cm^-3 and 1000 K, heated and ionised by
cosmic rays with the reduced network of geajay (primary ionisation rate
2e-16 s^-1, no metals, no UV background). The gas reaches an equilibrium
near 7900 K with an electron fraction near 0.03 within about 20 Myr.
The particles sit on a lattice, so every particle has the same state and
the box behaves as one zone.

Two parameter files give the same physics through two inputs:
  cosmicRayBox.yml             cosmic_ray_input: constant, one rate in the
                               parameter file;
  cosmicRayBoxPerParticle.yml  cosmic_ray_input: ionisation_rate, the rate is
                               stored in each particle (the field
                               CosmicRayValues of the snapshot).

checkEquilibrium.py compares the end state with a one-zone run of the Python
binding pygeajay. It tests how SWIFT passes the state, the units and the rate
to geajay. It does not test the cosmic-ray physics, because SWIFT and pygeajay
call the same solver. The bar and the size of the rate error that it detects
are in the header of the script. With --per-particle it also checks that
CosmicRayValues holds the rate, in the units of the snapshot metadata.

Configure SWIFT with a species network:
  ./configure --with-cooling=geajay_1 --with-geajay=$GEAJAY_ROOT

Run with ./run.sh. The comparison needs pygeajay (in the geajay source tree,
src/python) and GEAJAY_LIBRARY_PATH set to the same libgeajay.
